## Langfuse SDK majors add compressed export and remove deprecated APIs

Source: Langfuse · Hassieb Pakzad — https://langfuse.com/changelog/2026-10-14-python-sdk-v5-js-sdk-v6
Published: 2026-10-14
Channel: news

Langfuse released Python SDK v5 and JavaScript/TypeScript SDK v6 for its v4 platform. The announcement adds direct experiment-result links, typed metadata filtering, and gzip-compressed span batches. Numbers and booleans retain their types, while nested metadata can be filtered through dotted paths. Score creation and retrieval move into the current scores namespace; deprecated score namespaces and legacy API endpoints are removed. Both majors require Langfuse Cloud or self-hosted v4.59.0 or later, plus Python 3.11 or Node.js 22 and above. Existing v3 self-hosted deployments should retain the preceding SDK majors until upgrading.

For an instrumentation owner, compatibility is the immediate gate. Our editorial recommendation is to inventory removed API usage, check the deployed server version, and replay a representative trace before rollout. Include typed metadata, a score batch, and a large span export, then confirm the resulting records are queryable. Compression is a transport change, not evidence of lower end-to-end ingestion latency. Keep a rollback plan tied to compatible server and SDK versions so a migration does not quietly interrupt evaluation or trace collection.

Why it matters: Gate the SDK upgrade on server compatibility and an ingestion replay.

